# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for third-party code only.
# A line here hides a report. Do not add Emerald frames.

# SDL_Init starts PulseAudio's threaded mainloop (SDL_pulseaudio.c,
# PULSEAUDIO_Init). libpulse allocates a pa_mutex on its PulseMainloop thread
# and the thread that called SDL_Init locks it. TSan reports that as a data
# race in pa_mutex_lock / pa_xmalloc, inside libpulsecommon. Emerald never
# touches that mutex. Seen with the pinned SDL 3.4.16 and PulseAudio 16 on
# Ubuntu 24.04, under xvfb, and not on every run.
race:pa_mutex_lock

# Mesa lavapipe (the software Vulkan driver) races with itself when SDL creates
# a device. One llvmpipe thread calls pthread_mutex_destroy and another calls
# pthread_mutex_lock; both frames are inside libvulkan_lvp.so, reached from
# SDL's VULKAN_CreateDevice. The .so has no symbol names, so the pattern matches
# that library on the racing stack (TSan compares it to the module). Emerald's
# own frames are not in that library, so they still report.
race:libvulkan_lvp.so

# The same lavapipe device startup then races inside Mesa's LLVM JIT (libLLVM.so
# on the llvmpipe worker threads): bcmp in llvm::StringMapImpl::LookupBucketFor
# against memcpy, on a heap block from llvm::allocate_buffer. Emerald never calls
# LLVM. race:libvulkan_lvp.so does not see these frames; they are in libLLVM.
# Matches that module only (libLLVM.so.20.1 on Ubuntu 24.04).
race:libLLVM.so

# SDL's Vulkan backend locks two of its own mutexes in opposite orders while
# releasing the window (VULKAN_ReleaseWindow / VULKAN_INTERNAL_PerformPendingDestroys
# / VULKAN_INTERNAL_RemoveFramebuffersContainingView, all via SDL_LockMutex).
# Seen under TSan on shutdown after a lavapipe smoke run. Emerald only calls
# SDL_ReleaseWindowFromGPUDevice; it does not take those mutexes. Matches the
# SDL source file, not Emerald's.
deadlock:SDL_gpu_vulkan.c
